-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  dir_target.pcpp - Directive compliance: @target

  @target "<triple>"; names the target a program is built for: an LLVM
  triple, arch-vendor-os-env, or arch-os-env, which is the same target with
  LLVM's vendor for its OS (BNF.md sec 2). Only the two official targets are
  accepted: x86_64-pc-windows-gnu and x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu. It stands in
  the directive prologue (right after the module header), outside any @ifdef
  branch, before the first conditional directive, at most once. Only the main
  module's @target applies: an imported module may name the same target (in
  any spelling) and otherwise builds for the main module's.

  This file names Windows in four parts; the unit it imports names it in
  three (the same target: no report). dir_target_linux.pcpp names Linux in
  three parts and imports a unit with no @target. Each prints what PaskalCPP
  and clang build it for.

  How to check, from repo\bin (no -t: the file names its own target; a -t on
  the command line wins over every @target, this file's and its unit's):
    pcpp res\tests\dir_target.pcpp -r
  EXPECT:
    PaskalCPP builds for: x86_64-windows-gnu
    clang compiles for: _WIN32
    in the unit: x86_64-windows-gnu
    [Heap] Allocs: 0, Frees: 0, Leaked: 0
  EXITCODE: 0
  The Linux form: dir_target_linux.pcpp, checked the same way.

  Break it: run each file below the same way (no -t); each reports exactly
  what the // ERROR / WARN / HINT comment on its marked line says, and
  nothing else:
    dir_target_err_place.pcpp        @target after a declaration
    dir_target_err_branch.pcpp       @target inside an @ifdef branch
    dir_target_err_late.pcpp         @target after a conditional directive
    dir_target_err_duplicate.pcpp    @target given twice
    dir_target_err_string.pcpp       @target with a word, not a string
    dir_target_err_shape.pcpp        a triple of one part
    dir_target_err_unsupported.pcpp  a triple that is not an official target
    dir_target_err_mismatch.pcpp     an imported unit names another target
*******************************************************************************/

module exe dir_target;
@target "x86_64-pc-windows-gnu";
import dir_target_unit;

// The OS macro clang defines for the target it compiles for
static const char* dir_target_os()
{
  return ""
#ifdef _WIN32
  " _WIN32"
#endif
#ifdef __linux__
  " __linux__"
#endif
  ;
}

begin
@ifdef TARGET_X86_64_WINDOWS_GNU
  println("PaskalCPP builds for: x86_64-windows-gnu");
@endif
@ifdef TARGET_X86_64_LINUX_GNU
  println("PaskalCPP builds for: x86_64-linux-gnu");
@endif
  println("clang compiles for:{}", cpp("dir_target_os()"));
  dir_target_unit.ShowTarget();
end.
